<div class="page-head">
  <div class="page-head-row page-head-row--split page-head-row--stacked">
    <h1 class="section-title"><span class="gradient-text">About</span></h1>
    <%= link_to "Run Job", new_job_path, class: "btn btn-primary head-action" %>

  <p class="page-head-blurb">What this thing is, what it is made of, and how you drive it. It traces a picture into vector candidates, draws or renders geometry, generates the sources it traces, and measures all of it against the rules a real screen-printer cares about. It never picks a winner — you choose, and your pick gets proved.</p>
  </div></div>

<%# #about-consolidation
    Rewritten from sixteen flat <h3> sections into the five a new operator actually
    needs, in the order they need them. Two things changed beyond the reordering,
    and both are deliberate:

    1. THE DETAIL MOVED TO WHERE IT IS EDITED. Every per-service surface -- the
       flux model registry, bp's five plot operations, bp's blender document
       vocabulary, the job-contract keys, the ML/ranking constants -- is now a
       paragraph that says what the surface IS and links to the page that carries
       the detail in a popup. Those popups are the copy that gets kept in step
       with the code, because they sit next to the control they describe. Restating
       600 lines of registry prose here is how a help page rots: this file is
       orientation and navigation, and the runner pages are reference.
    2. THE UNLINKABLE SURFACES ARE NOW LINKS. The scrapbook, the composite
       builder, the tournament and the datasets are all real, reachable pages --
       but none of them is in the nav, so an operator arriving here had four
       features described in prose and no way to find them. Each is now a link.
       Filed as an issue, because the nav itself is a separate decision.

    Nothing was dropped that a reader needed: every rule, threshold, refusal and
    "it will not do" line survives, either here or behind the link it belongs to.
    The P1-P5 breakdown stays in full -- it is the answer to "why is this
    candidate first", which is the single most-asked question the page exists for. %>

<%# ---- 1. WHAT IT IS ---------------------------------------------------------------- %>
<div class="card" data-about-is>
  <h3>It is a print shop that shows its working</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    You give it a picture — a logo, a scan, an AI-generated image. It produces a set of
    traced vector candidates, measures each against the rules a real screen-printer cares
    about (colour count, node weight, stroke thickness, ink coverage), and shows you the
    trade-offs. It never picks a winner. Tracing quality is a visual judgment — you choose
    from the candidates and the metrics, and your pick is then
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">proved</strong>: a proof image, a print PDF, and a
    manifest recording every tool version and measurement.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    The measuring happens in two layers, and the split is the point.
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Layer A</strong> reads the SVG
    <em>source</em>: declared colours against budget (an implicit black fill counts, a
    <span class="mono">currentColor</span> is resolved from the inherited colour, content
    hidden by <span class="mono">display:none</span> is not counted at all), palette
    conformance, node counts, stroke widths, gradients refused outright
    (<span class="mono">GRADIENT_NOT_ALLOWED</span>), and any bitmap hidden in the vector
    (<span class="mono">RASTER_EMBED</span> — an <span class="mono">&lt;image&gt;</span>,
    an <span class="mono">&lt;feImage&gt;</span>, a
    <span class="mono">&lt;foreignObject&gt;</span>, a reference to another document, or
    a raster paint server). What it cannot measure from the source it does not pretend to
    clear: a <span class="mono">filter</span>, <span class="mono">mask</span> or
    <span class="mono">clip-path</span> reference raises the advisory
    <span class="mono">EFFECT_REFERENCE</span>.
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Layer B</strong> renders the file and
    measures the truth: real ink count after merging near-identical colours, total area
    coverage, gradient detection in the render.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    Candidates sort by fewest hard gates failed, then fewest advisories — nothing more.
    Pixel fidelity (how close the trace renders to your original) is a second, independent
    lens, and the two are never combined into a single verdict.
  </p>
  <div class="actions-row">
    <span class="tech-tag">prep → sweep → compare</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">you pick</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">proof · Layer A + Layer B</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">never picks for you</span>
  </div>
</div>

<%# ---- 2. WHAT IT IS MADE OF -------------------------------------------------------- %>
<div class="card" data-about-made-of>
  <h3>Five programs, and which one owns what</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    The GUI never runs the pipeline itself. It talks to a <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">runner</strong>
    over HTTP, and a runner is a URL and a kind. One rule covers all of them: this layer
    calls the pipeline, and the pipeline never calls it back. And no runner judges the art
    — it produces, a human picks. The contract lives in
    <span class="mono">docs/runner-contract.md</span>.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    Only an <span class="mono">svg</span> runner is a hand-off target: the other kinds
    trace nothing and hold no job state. Tracing is <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">VTracer
    only</strong> — never an LLM, never hand-drawn paths.
  </p>
  <div class="actions-row">
    <span class="tech-tag">svg computes the ML</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">studio presents and owns metrics</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">bp draws and renders</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">bp scores nothing</span>
  </div>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    Read the ownership carefully, because it is the thing most often got wrong:
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">chopshop-svg</strong> (the pipeline)
    computes the machine learning — the learned ranker, the bucket bandit and the triage
    gates — and is the source of truth for those constants.
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">chopshop-studio</strong> (this app) owns the
    metrics engine: the configuration of that ML, and the presentation of server statistics
    — the footer's check-engine light, the STATUS page it leads to, and
    <span class="mono">GET /engine.json</span>. The studio does not compute the ML; it
    records the signal the pipeline trains on, reports the shipped constants verbatim, and
    serves the counts. <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">chopshop-bp</strong> is the
    service the studio calls for blend-plot work; it applies no scoring, keeps no telemetry,
    and never picks a winner. Drawing a document is itself a blend-plot task, so the STATUS
    charts being drawn by bp is the intended division of labour, not a computation that
    belongs here.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    One thing to read carefully: <span class="mono">metrics_version: 1</span> is a shared
    flat <em>data contract</em> — the shape of the run records the chart operations read —
    not a scoring engine. Nothing on this page or in bp ranks, scores or recommends.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="grid-2" style="align-items:stretch;margin-top:1rem;">
  <%# Each card below states what the service IS and sends the detail to the page
      that owns it, where it lives in a popup beside the control it describes. %>
  <div class="card" data-about-svc-svg>
    <h3><span class="mono">chopshop-svg</span> — the pipeline</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      The real tracing pipeline, and the only hand-off target. It preps the raster, sweeps
      VTracer across many parameter sets, scores every candidate against the gates, learns
      which candidates an operator preferred, and trains the bucket bandit. It owns the
      ML constants, the event log it trains on, and the learned files.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      It is configured by the job contract you compose on the
      <%= link_to "JSON page", json_path %> — every key, what it gates, and the shipped
      default is in the popup beside each one.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">prep · sweep · compare</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">gates · ranker · bandit</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">the only hand-off target</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-about-svc-flux>
    <h3><span class="mono">chopshop-flux</span> — generation</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Generates the sources you trace, behind its own small standard API
      (<span class="mono">POST /generate</span> and <span class="mono">/edit</span>, plus
      <span class="mono">/models</span>, <span class="mono">/health</span> and
      <span class="mono">/files/&lt;name&gt;</span>) — replacing the old SwarmUI/ComfyUI
      integration. The registry is no longer one family: <%= FluxConf::MODELS.size %>
      checkpoints are registered
    (<%= FluxConf::MODELS.keys.join(" · ") %>) and every one of them loads. The default is
    <span class="mono"><%= FluxConf::DEFAULT_MODEL_KEY %></span>.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Two licensing notes that decide what you may sell: the default and
    <span class="mono">sdxl-1.0</span> and <span class="mono">sd-1.5</span> carry
    commercial terms, while <span class="mono">sdxl-turbo</span> is Stability AI
    Non-Commercial and only loads once
    <span class="mono">CHOPSHOP_FLUX_ACK_NONCOMMERCIAL=1</span> is set.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      The full registry — every model, its measured steps and guidance, VRAM, licence, and
      the per-family pipeline class — is on the <%= link_to "FLUX page", flux_path %>, in
      the registry popup, which mirrors
      <span class="mono">chopshop-flux</span>'s <span class="mono">models.py</span>.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">raster sources</span>
      <span class="tech-tag"><%= FluxConf::MODELS.size %> models</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">licence gates the load</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-about-svc-bp>
    <h3><span class="mono">chopshop-bp</span> — Blend-Plot, two halves</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      ONE program that draws documents and renders geometry in 3D, on one port with one
      token. Its repo was renamed <span class="mono">chopshop-plot</span> →
      <span class="mono">chopshop-bp</span> precisely because it hosts both halves, so it
      gets ONE page with a Plot section and a Blender section rather than two pages
      pretending to be two services.
      <span class="mono">/plot</span> and <span class="mono">/blender</span> redirect to
      it, so an old link still lands somewhere.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Plot</strong> is the document half: one JSON
      document in, one SVG out — the geometry spec, the metric charts and the run records,
      plus the event log the STATUS page draws. Five operations ship: a geometry
      <span class="mono">generate</span>, a <span class="mono">digest</span> chart, a
      cross-run <span class="mono">query</span>, a hierarchical
      <span class="mono">tree</span>, and the <span class="mono">log</span> timeline. Every
      operation refuses with a JSON envelope naming what was wrong rather than drawing
      something else.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Blender</strong> is the 3D half: a geometry
      document in, rendered with headless Blender — the frame itself, or a manifest of
      frames plus an optional GLB. A closed contour becomes a filled bevelled solid; an
      open element becomes a stroke tube whose radius is its
      <span class="mono">stroke_width</span>. A runner without Blender installed answers
      <span class="mono">503</span>: a state a deployment is allowed to be in, not a fault.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      The five operations, the chart shapes, the metric field registry and the Blender
      document vocabulary are on the <%= link_to "BP page", bp_path %>, in the popups in its
      Plot and Blender sections — which mirror
      <span class="mono">chopshop-bp</span>'s <span class="mono">config.py</span>,
      <span class="mono">metrics.py</span> and <span class="mono">spec.py</span>.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">one service</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">plot draws documents</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">blend renders 3D</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">five operations</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">the axis is yours</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-about-svc-jev>
    <h3><span class="mono">chopshop-jev</span> — System One</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      After a proof, you can ask <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Jev</strong>
      (TypeSafe's System One model) four typed advisory questions: dominant issue, next
      action, evidence quality, and whether a human should eyeball the proof. It answers
      with typed decisions in a few hundred milliseconds — no prose, no hallucination
      surface. It never overrides the gates and never picks a candidate.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      The decision types and what each one is allowed to assert are on the
      <%= link_to "JEV page", jev_path %>, in the popups beside each field.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">typed decisions</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">advisory only</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">no prose</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<%# ---- 3. HOW YOU USE IT ------------------------------------------------------------ %>
<div class="card" data-about-use>
  <h3>How you drive it</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    Everything lives in a <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">project</strong> —
    the landing page is the Projects index, and a project's workspace is where its jobs,
    composites and outputs sit. Inside one, press
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Run Job</strong>, start from a
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">template</strong> when the picker offers
    one, give it a picture and a
    runner, and the studio does none of the work itself: it uploads your image, writes a
    job record in that project, and hands the runner a spec. Everything below happens on the runner; this
    page just polls for the result. A job moves through
    <span class="mono"><%= Job::STATES.join(" → ") %></span>, and while it is
    <%= Job::IN_FLIGHT_STATES.join(" / ") %> the page polls the runner for a log tail. You
    can cancel a long run, and delete a finished one.
  </p>
  <ol style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;padding-left:1.2rem;">
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Upload</strong> — PNG or JPEG, ≤ 50MB,
      magic-byte checked. The studio never decodes it; the bytes are proxied straight
      through to the runner.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Prep</strong> — the raster is made
      pipeline-legal: format and colour-mode checks, aspect ratio reported (never silently
      stretched), optional background removal and upscale. Prep is
      <em>check-first</em>: it reports what it would change and only rewrites when asked.
      It also writes a <span class="mono">colour-inverted twin</span> of your source, so you
      can compare how a design reads on black.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Sweep</strong> — VTracer runs the same
      image under many parameter sets (presets, speckle filtering, palette depth,
      node-preservation modes), producing a dozen candidate SVGs. Two of the levers are
      genuinely orthogonal: one moves the <em>colour count</em>, the other moves the
      <em>worst single path's node count</em>. Neither alone gets a file under the node
      budget, so the interesting configurations are the 2-D ones.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Compare</strong> — every candidate is
      scored on the gates, and (optionally) rendered back to a raster and diffed against
      your original. That fidelity number is measured over <em>artwork</em> pixels only — an
      overall average is dominated by the background and flatters bad traces.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">You pick</strong> — from the table, the
      preview tiles, and the metrics. Nothing advances on its own.</li>
    <li><strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Validate</strong> — Layer A + Layer B on
      your pick, producing a proof PNG, <span class="mono">print.pdf</span>, and a manifest
      recording every tool version and measurement.</li>
  </ol>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    A <span class="mono">flux</span> or <span class="mono">bp</span> job does one thing
    instead of six — it makes a source, or draws or renders the one document that IS the
    deliverable. (A job filed against a legacy <span class="mono">plot</span> or
    <span class="mono">blender</span> label still runs: those kinds were folded into
    <span class="mono">bp</span> rather than removed, so nothing already filed broke.) The
    runner also keeps a disk-capacity gate, so a run that would fill the volume stops
    itself rather than wedging the box.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="grid-2" style="align-items:stretch;margin-top:1rem;">
  <div class="card" data-about-sequences>
    <h3>Several actions in one job</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      A job is normally ONE action. Ticking <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Sequence</strong>
      on the Run Job page makes it an ORDERED LIST instead — a generation, a trace, a render,
      in the order you put them — and the whole list is still one job, one queue slot, one
      record. The sequence rides in the job's contract under
      <span class="mono">steps</span>, so a hand-written contract still wins and there is one
      place a document is defined rather than a second copy to keep in step. A contract with
      no <span class="mono">steps</span> key is a single-action job exactly as it always was.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">The studio drives the chain, not the
      runner.</strong> A runner is stateless and serves one operation per request — it holds
      no idea that a chain exists. So the studio posts step one, waits, then posts step two
      with step one's output handed in. That is why a sequence needs no runner change at all,
      and why the order in the list IS the order they run. A step that cannot run without a
      source takes the file the step before produced, when that is a file it accepts; a
      render step handed a drawing has it converted into the render's own geometry
      vocabulary. Where a hand-off cannot be worked out the sequence is refused and says
      which step it could not resolve, rather than running something with no input.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      If a step fails the chain stops there and the job says which one it was; finished steps
      keep their output. Cancelling stops it between steps. Untick the box and the job is exactly
      the single action it always was.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">one job, one slot</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">order is the list order</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">refused, not guessed</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-about-jobs-list>
    <h3>Reading a project's jobs list</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Runs live in their project's workspace — open a project from the landing
      Projects page. Two views of the same runs, and a toggle for them.
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">List</strong> is the table — runner and
      passed-count columns, one row per run. <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Grid</strong>
      is the same runs as thumbnail cards: one picture, the name, the state, the passed
      count, and the actions. The grid is deliberately low-information, so a card stays a
      picture you recognise rather than a paragraph. The choice is remembered in the browser.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      The card actions are icons: <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">preview</strong>
      (the same popup the job page opens), <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">download</strong>
      (the finished run's <span class="mono">7z</span> archive),
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">send to Run Job</strong> (reuse the source
      raster), <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">save to the scrapbook</strong>, and
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">cancel or delete</strong> — cancel while a run
      is in flight, delete once it is not. They are inline SVG rather than text glyphs on
      purpose: the pixel font does not contain them, so a glyph silently falls back to
      another face and draws at a different size inside the same box.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">List / Grid</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">icon actions</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">remembered</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-about-comparison>
    <h3>Reading a comparison table</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">hard</strong> — a rule the cutter will not
      forgive (too many colours, banned embed, a physical-size violation). A hard failure is
      a stop.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">advisory</strong> — worth knowing, not
      fatal. Treat these as the shortlist filter.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">MAE_art</strong> — mean pixel error over
      artwork pixels only. Lower is closer to your original. It is a
      <em>measurement</em>, and two candidates can tie on it while looking completely
      different. It ranks rows; it does not choose.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">PALETTE</strong> — the trace's real colours,
      which frequently are not the ones you asked for. Read this before trusting any
      colour-sorted column.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-about-node-weight>
    <h3>When a trace is still too heavy</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      A cutter has a limit on how many points a single path may carry. A trace can clear
      every colour and ink rule and still be unprintable purely because one path has too many
      nodes. That case is remediable, but only by simplifying that specific path — rewriting
      the whole file makes things measurably worse.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Simplification is easy to do badly. Every failure mode found while building it looked
      like a <em>success</em>: an unsupported curve quietly deleted a shape, two separate
      shapes quietly welded into one, a closed path quietly lost its fill closure. All of
      them still reported a lower node count. So the tool refuses to accept a fit it cannot
      verify, and reports what it declined. The number to watch is not the node count but the
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">rendered result</strong>: the same file can
      clear its budget while visibly eroding. Proof image, not gate, is the pass condition.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<%# The four surfaces that used to be described in prose here and were unreachable
    from the nav. Each is a real page; each is now a link. The nav gap itself is
    filed as an issue rather than fixed here, because the nav is a layout decision
    and not this page's to make. %>
<div class="grid-2" style="align-items:stretch;margin-top:1rem;">
  <div class="card" data-about-scrapbook>
    <h3>The Scrapbook</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      A drawer down the left edge of every page, and the operator's own store of things
      worth keeping. Star a source raster, its inverted twin, a candidate or a proof
      anywhere it appears and it lands here. Generated rasters and flattened composites land
      here too. Reached at <%= link_to "the scrapbook", scrapbook_path %>.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">A copy, not a reference.</strong> The bytes
      are copied into the scrapbook's own storage, so deleting the job they came from leaves
      the scrapbook intact. That is the whole requirement, and it is why the item carries its
      own <span class="mono">sha256</span>: content is the identity, so the same bytes saved
      twice are one item rather than two. Every item can be sent back to Run Job — as the
      artwork, or to FLUX as a reference image if it is a raster.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">survives the job</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">copy, not reference</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">back to Run Job</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-about-composite>
    <h3>The Composite builder</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Assemble a source raster out of layered pieces on a canvas, at
      <%= link_to "the builder", new_composite_path %>. A layer is not a file: it references
      where its pixels came from — a scrapbook item, an upload, a blank slate — plus the
      placement that makes it a component (x/y/w/h, opacity, hue, stacking order). The parts
      stay editable for as long as the composite exists.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">Flatten is the one server-side step.</strong>
      A raster-only composite is composed in the browser for its preview, but the runner only
      reads uploads, and a canvas is not a file — so the composed PNG is posted to the server,
      which stores it twice: once as a scrapbook item (the re-usable artefact) and once as the
      upload the job will use. It is routed through the scrapbook deliberately: one store for
      "things I made" is simpler than two.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">A vector layer is never rasterised.</strong>
      The moment a composite holds one, the studio posts the layers to the svg runner's
      <span class="mono">POST /compose</span>, which merges the vectors themselves, so a
      traced source stays a trace instead of being flattened into pixels and traced a second
      time. The default canvas is <span class="mono">3000 × 3000</span>, transparent.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">layers, not files</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">flatten on the server</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">vectors stay vectors</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">via the scrapbook</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-about-tournament>
    <h3>The Tournament</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      A bracket for deciding between finished candidates, at
      <%= link_to "the tournament page", tournament_path %>. It is the one place the app
      compares candidates head to head, and — like everything else here — it stops at your
      pick. The bracket is computed and drawn in Ruby on that page rather than asked of the
      Plot service's <span class="mono">tree</span> operation.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">head to head</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">you still pick</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-about-datasets>
    <h3>Datasets</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Curated source material to trace rather than upload, at
      <%= link_to "the datasets page", datasets_path %>. The library, draw, meme-label and
      surface sets are each their own view, and picking one hands its entry to the new-job
      form as the artwork.
    </p>
    <div class="actions-row">
      <span class="tech-tag">library · draw · meme-labels · surface</span>
      <span class="tech-tag">hands off to Run Job</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<%# ---- 4. WHY THE ORDER IS WHAT IT IS ----------------------------------------------- %>
<div class="card" data-about-ranking>
  <h3>Why the candidates are in that order</h3>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    The order candidates are shown in is not a mystery, and it is not a black box that
    chooses for you. Five things make it up, and every one of them is owner-scoped: one
    operator's actions are never another operator's training data.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">P1 · hard gates.</strong> Thresholds with
    shipped defaults —
    <span class="mono">max_unique_colors 8 · max_speckles 40 · max_glow_area_pct 8 ·
    min_silhouette_iou 0.85 · min_luma_delta 12 · weak_needed 3 · bucket_cap 2</span>.
    A <em>hard</em> gate drops a candidate on any hit; a <em>weak</em> gate demotes it once
    the <span class="mono">weak_needed</span> hits accumulate. No verdict deletes anything —
    a drop is a discard, and the human can still act on it.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">P2 · the append-only event log.</strong> Every
    star, unstar, pick and delete is a row, carrying the artifact's checksum, the position it
    held in the ordered list, and which ranker proposed that order. Positions are 1-based on
    both sides, so exposure and action join on one scale. The log has no foreign key to jobs —
    a row is provenance, not a child — so the signal outlives the job it describes.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">P3 · text similarity.</strong> A pure-Python
    bigram/TF-IDF cosine over prompts, filenames, tags and finding text, kept deterministic
    and digit-preserving, so two parameter sets that differ only in a number are not read as
    the same thing.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">P4 · the learned pairwise ranker.</strong> It
    learns which candidate a human preferred, from within-job pairs, and only once the log
    holds <span class="mono">learn_min_picks=30</span> picks across
    <span class="mono">learn_min_jobs=2</span> jobs. Below either threshold the
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">hand order</strong> stands — fewest hard gates,
    then advisories, then artwork MAE, with a candidate missing a reading sorted last. Its
    features include the gate counts, node count, artwork MAE, colour and speckle counts,
    bucketed bins and a one-hot per preset. And it is deliberately kept honest: 1 slot of the
    visible top-8 is held back for a random or uncertain candidate
    (<span class="mono">n_random_slots=1</span>, <span class="mono">window=8</span>), so the
    order a human acts on is never fed back as its own answer.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">P5 · the bucket bandit.</strong> Beta-Bernoulli
    Thompson sampling decides which bucket to try next — over bucket keys, never over
    candidates. One draw in ten explores a bucket the statistics do not favour
    (<span class="mono">epsilon 0.1</span>), bucket statistics expire after 30 days so the mix
    follows the sources in front of you, and a veto list removes a bucket outright whatever
    its sampled score.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">It never picks a winner.</strong> The ranker
    and the bandit <em>order</em> and <em>propose</em>; the human picks. The learned files
    (<span class="mono">learned_ranker.json</span>, <span class="mono">learned_buckets.json</span>)
    live in the pipeline's learning dir, because the pipeline trains — this studio records the
    signal and serves it at <span class="mono">GET /events</span>.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
    Every constant above, verbatim as the pipeline ships it, is in the
    <strong style="color:var(--text-primary)">ML / RANKING</strong> popup on the
    <%= link_to "STATUS page", status_path %> — reached from the footer's check-engine light,
    which is amber until there is any signal to learn from and green once there is. The
    recorded signal in counts and the event log drawn from it are on the same page.
  </p>
  <div class="actions-row">
    <span class="tech-tag">P1 gates</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">P2 event log</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">P3 text similarity</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">P4 pairwise ranker</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">P5 bandit</span>
    <span class="tech-tag">it never picks a winner</span>
  </div>
</div>

<%# ---- 5. THE END GAME -------------------------------------------------------------- %>
<div class="grid-2" style="align-items:stretch;margin-top:1rem;">
  <div class="card" data-about-privacy>
    <h3>How this stays private</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Each user signs in with a password — their password IS their identity, and it gates
      access to their own jobs, candidates, proofs, scrapbook and composites; no one sees
      anyone else's work. The same scoping covers the learning signal: the event log, the
      telemetry counts and the model trained on them are per-operator, so one person's choices
      are never another's training data. Failed attempts are logged with timestamps and the
      reference deployment runs <span class="mono">fail2ban</span> against that log. Runners
      are never bound to a public interface.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      Search engines are declined twice over: <span class="mono">robots.txt</span> disallows
      everything, and every response carries <span class="mono">X-Robots-Tag: noindex</span>.
      The password is the actual protection — the other two are a request, and a crawler that
      ignores robots.txt will ignore this too.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="card" data-about-will-not>
    <h3>Where it stops</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;">
      These are not gaps to be filled later. Each is a boundary the design holds on purpose,
      and the reason is the same in every case: a measurement you cannot verify, or a judgment
      that is not the machine's to make, is reported rather than faked.
    </p>
    <ul style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin-top:0.75rem;padding-left:1.2rem;">
      <li>Pick a candidate for you, or advance one to a proof on its own — not the studio,
        not the ranker, not the bandit.</li>
      <li>Trace, validate, render, or generate anything itself — that is the runner's job,
        and the boundary is deliberate.</li>
      <li>Generate or edit SVG paths with an LLM.</li>
      <li>Hardcode a chart axis, rank the metric fields, or plot a missing measurement as a
        zero.</li>
      <li>Stretch artwork to fit a spec. An aspect mismatch is reported, never "fixed" by
        distortion.</li>
      <li>Prescribe a print size. Add dimensions to a spec only when the job has a fixed
        physical size.</li>
      <li>Let one operator's actions train on another's work.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>
